@page "/config"
@implements IDisposable
@inject LightingEngineHost Engine
@inject HaCatalog Catalog
@inject ConfigLocation Location
@inject HomeLocation HomeGeo
@inject AreaSnapshotCache Cache
@inject ILogger<ConfigEditor> Logger

@*
	The House tab — everything that isn't one room: what every room starts with, the day's schedule, the modes,
	the people, and the engine itself.

	It used to be a viewer, which meant it was an ornament: the thing it displayed could only be changed by
	editing YAML over SSH and restarting the host, so the page's own existence made nothing possible. It now
	reads and writes the document, validates before writing, and rebuilds the running engine in place afterwards.

	The page has four sections — Areas, Schedule, House modes, House — and shows one at a time behind a section
	rail rather than stacking them into one endless scroll: on a phone the old page was several screens of
	unrelated knobs before the house-wide settings even began. The sections are named after things a person can
	point at, not after where a value is stored, and anything rarely touched folds away at the bottom of its
	section rather than into a top-level "Advanced" drawer.

	WHAT DECIDES WHICH SECTION. Areas is a list of rooms. House holds the settings there is exactly one of — the
	defaults every room starts with, how lights and sensors are found, the house's name, its people, the master
	switch, the fine tuning. Schedule and House modes are their own sections because they are big and distinct.
	The earlier rule was "a setting lives under the noun it changes", which put the all-rooms defaults and the
	discovery labels in Areas; it does not discriminate, because the schedule and the modes change how rooms
	behave too. See the comment above the House section for the whole argument.

	THE DISCLOSURE MODEL, the same one the room page uses. Each card leads with the thing said as English, with
	the values inline and clickable, and keeps the full detail underneath for somebody who came for a specific
	knob: the all-rooms defaults are four sentences over an All-settings reveal, the schedule is a chart and a
	blend sentence over the period table, and each house mode is one sentence over the field-by-field editor.
	Overlap between a sentence and the rows below it is intended — the sentence is how somebody reads the house,
	the rows are how somebody finds a setting by what it changes.

	THE ROOMS ARE A LIST, NOT AN EDITOR. Each room is one row that opens its own page at /room/{areaId}, where
	its live state, its evidence and all twenty-one of its settings are. The inline per-room editor this page
	used to host is gone: two editors for one room could not stay equivalent, and did not — the old one offered
	no rows at all for the five daylight-brightness settings, so a room tuned on the new page had values the old
	one could neither show nor change. What the list keeps is what only a list can do: the power switch on each
	room, the live-state edge colour, the floor grouping and its per-floor bulk switch, and the two acts that
	are about the set of rooms rather than about one of them.

	THE WRITE PATH — deliberately still the save bar, where the room page chose to debounce.
	A room-page edit changes one room; every edit here changes something every room inherits, and Save rebuilds
	every area controller in the house. Three things follow from that and all three point the same way:

	  - The batch is real. "Switch on this floor" is six rooms in one press, and "Set up rooms again" rebuilds
	    several at once — its warning dialog says in so many words that nothing is written until Save and that
	    Discard changes undoes it. Instant apply would make an owner-approved warning untrue.
	  - Not every control here is constrained. The house name, the device-class set and the write-only NetDaemon
	    user id are free text, and the argument that makes per-change apply safe elsewhere ("no interaction can
	    produce an invalid document") does not hold for them. The user id in particular is applied on save by
	    design, and a debounce would write it half-typed.
	  - Regret costs more. A mis-dragged default is live in seventeen rooms, and Discard changes is a stronger
	    answer than a one-deep undo: it puts the whole document back to what is on disk.

	So the save bar stays, it stays sticky, and every token popover says what it promises — applies when you
	save, Discard changes puts it back. No new write path either way: Save goes to LightingEngineHost, which
	validates, writes one file, re-reads it and rebuilds the engine.

	Two properties this page must never lose:

	  - Token safety. It binds AdaptiveLightingConfig and nothing else. It never injects IConfiguration, and
	    it must never be widened to render one: the root configuration carries the Home Assistant long-lived
	    token, and this document does not. NetDaemonUserId is in the document, and is rendered as set/not-set
	    only — never its value, in either direction.
	  - One write surface. Save goes to LightingEngineHost, which writes exactly one file whose path was
	    resolved server-side at start-up. Nothing on this page names a path, and nothing it posts can.
*@

<PageTitle>The house &mdash; Adaptive lighting</PageTitle>

<h1>The house</h1>

@if (_loadError is { Length: > 0 })
{
	<div class="notice bad">
		<strong>The configuration file couldn't be read.</strong>
		<p>@_loadError</p>
		<p>
			The file is <code>@Location.Path</code>. Fix it by hand, or press <em>Start from scratch</em> &mdash;
			the old file is kept as <code>@Engine.Store.BackupPath</code> when you save.
		</p>
		<button type="button" @onclick="StartFromScratch">Start from scratch</button>
	</div>
}
else
{
	<p class="lede">
		Everything that isn't one room: what every room starts with, the day's schedule, the modes, the people
		and the engine itself. Changes apply as soon as you save &mdash; no restart needed.
	</p>

	@* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	   Where the file lives, and whether a deploy will eat it. Worth a permanent panel rather than a
	   footnote: a person editing a document that the next publish silently reverts would conclude the
	   UI is broken, and would be right.
	   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
	@if (Location.Warning is { Length: > 0 })
	{
		<div class="notice warn">
			<strong>Edits here will not survive a deploy.</strong>
			<p>@Location.Warning</p>
		</div>
	}

	@*
		The engine and the connection, above the rail and only when one of them is wrong.

		The shipped page carried a three-row status panel on every visit to say that everything was fine, which
		is the same reassurance budget the board's chrome was spending. What could not move into a fold is the
		part that is a problem report: a stopped engine and an unreachable Home Assistant both explain why an
		edit did not reach the house, and an explanation that hides with a section somebody is not looking at
		explains nothing. The full detail — the file, its backup, the managed-room count — lives under House.
	*@
	@if (!Engine.IsRunning || !HomeAssistantIsAnswering)
	{
		<div class="notice warn">
			@if (!Engine.IsRunning)
			{
				<div><strong>Automatic lighting isn't running.</strong> @(Engine.Fault ?? "Not started yet.") Edits here are saved either way, and take effect when it starts.</div>
			}
			@if (!HomeAssistantIsAnswering)
			{
				<div>
					<strong>Waiting for Home Assistant.</strong> The pickers below have nothing to offer until it answers.
					You can still type ids by hand; name checks are skipped if you save now.
				</div>
			}
		</div>
	}

	@* --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
	<div class="savebar">
		<button type="button" class="primary" @onclick="Save" disabled="@_busy">Save and apply</button>
		<button type="button" @onclick="Reload" disabled="@_busy">Discard changes</button>

		@* Validation errors and a refused/failed save stay put until they are resolved — they are the reason the
		   document is not safe, and a message that names the problem must not vanish on a timer. Only the SUCCESS
		   confirmation is transient: it is rendered separately below and auto-dismisses. *@
		@if (_liveValidation is { IsValid: false })
		{
			<span class="savebar-note bad">
				@_liveValidation.Errors.Count @(_liveValidation.Errors.Count == 1 ? "problem" : "problems") to fix before saving.
			</span>
		}
		else if (_result is { Written: false } result)
		{
			<span class="savebar-note bad">@result.Message</span>
		}

		@if (_saveConfirmation is { Length: > 0 } confirmation)
		{
			<span class="savebar-note ok savebar-toast" role="status">@confirmation</span>
		}
	</div>

	@* The validator already returns precise, actionable messages. They are shown verbatim: restating them
	   here would mean two descriptions of one rule, and the one on this page would be the one that rots.
	   They also sit above the section rail on purpose — an error in a section you are not looking at
	   still refuses the save, so it must not be able to hide with its section. *@
	@if (_liveValidation is { } validation && (validation.Errors.Count > 0 || validation.AreaErrors.Count > 0))
	{
		<div class="notice @(validation.IsValid ? "warn" : "bad")">
			@if (validation.Errors.Count > 0)
			{
				<strong>Fix these before saving:</strong>
				<ul>
					@foreach (string error in validation.Errors)
					{
						<li>@error</li>
					}
				</ul>
			}
			@if (validation.AreaErrors.Count > 0)
			{
				<strong>These rooms will be skipped &mdash; everything else still works, and you can still save:</strong>
				<ul>
					@foreach (AreaError areaError in validation.AreaErrors)
					{
						<li><b>@areaError.AreaName</b>: @areaError.Message</li>
					}
				</ul>
			}
		</div>
	}

	<div class="config-layout">
		@* On a desktop this is a rail beside the content; on a phone it collapses to a row of chips
		   above it — a thumb finds a chip, not a cramped sidebar. *@
		<nav class="config-nav" aria-label="Configuration sections">
			<button type="button" class="config-nav-link @(ActiveClass(Section.Areas))" @onclick="() => Show(Section.Areas)">
				Areas <span class="config-nav-count">@_config.Areas.Count</span>
			</button>
			<button type="button" class="config-nav-link @(ActiveClass(Section.Schedule))" @onclick="() => Show(Section.Schedule)">
				Schedule <span class="config-nav-count">@_config.Periods.Count</span>
			</button>
			<button type="button" class="config-nav-link @(ActiveClass(Section.HouseModes))" @onclick="() => Show(Section.HouseModes)">
				House modes
				@if (_houseModeOptionValues.Count > 0)
				{
					<span class="config-nav-count">@_houseModeOptionValues.Count</span>
				}
			</button>
			<button type="button" class="config-nav-link @(ActiveClass(Section.House))" @onclick="() => Show(Section.House)">
				House
			</button>
		</nav>

		<div class="config-body">
			@if (_section == Section.Areas)
			{
				<header class="section-head">
					<h2>Areas</h2>
					<p class="lede">
						Rooms not listed here are never touched. Lights and sensors are found automatically from Home
						Assistant's areas &mdash; open a room to see what was found and to change how it behaves.
						@* The two groups that moved out of here. A thread rather than a silent absence: somebody who
						   knew where they were has to be told where they went, once, on the page they left. *@
						What every room starts with, and which lights count as a room's, are under
						<button type="button" class="thread" @onclick="() => Show(Section.House)">House</button>.
					</p>
				</header>

				<div class="panel-actions">
					<button type="button" @onclick="ToggleAddRoom" disabled="@(_setupScope is not null)">
						@(_addingRoom ? "Cancel" : "Add a room")
					</button>
					<button type="button" @onclick="OpenSetupForEveryRoom" disabled="@(!CanSetUpAgain)">Set up rooms again</button>
				</div>

				@if (!HomeAssistantIsAnswering)
				{
					<p class="hint">
						Setting rooms up again needs Home Assistant &mdash; each room is rebuilt from what the registry
						says right now.
					</p>
				}

				@*
					Adding a room is picking one of the Home Assistant areas that has no room yet. It used to append a
					blank row whose only purpose was to make you choose an area inside it; asking first means the room
					arrives already knowing what it is, and a room that already exists is never offered twice.
				*@
				@if (_addingRoom)
				{
					<div class="panel house-add">
						<div class="field">
							<div class="field-head"><label class="field-label">Which room?</label></div>
							<div class="field-control">
								<AreaPicker Value="@_addAreaId"
											ValueChanged="@AddRoom"
											Areas="@_addableAreas"
											NoneLabel="(pick a room)"
											EmptyNote="@AddEmptyNote" />
							</div>
							<div class="field-help">
								It arrives switched off, so nothing in it changes until you switch it on.
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				}

				@if (_setupNote is { Length: > 0 } setupNote)
				{
					<div class="notice ok">@setupNote</div>
				}

				@if (_setupScope is { } scope)
				{
					<SetupAgainPanel Config="@_config"
									 PreTicked="@scope"
									 PlanFor="@PlanSetup"
									 NameOf="@AreaName"
									 Confirmed="@ApplySetup"
									 Cancelled="@CloseSetup" />
				}

				@* Floors come from the registry through the shared grouping helper, so this list and the board's lanes
				   can never disagree about which room is upstairs. A house with no floors gets one unnamed group and
				   therefore no headers at all — exactly the flat list it had before floors existed. *@
				@foreach (FloorGroup<AreaConfig> group in _areaGroups)
				{
					FloorGroup<AreaConfig> floor = group;

					@if (AreaView.ShowsHeader(_areaGroups.Count, floor.Floor))
					{
						<div class="floor-head">
							<span class="floor-name">@AreaView.FloorTitle(floor.Floor)</span>
							<button type="button" class="floor-bulk" @onclick="() => SwitchFloor(floor)">@FloorAction(floor)</button>
						</div>
					}

					<div class="room-rows">
						@foreach (AreaConfig area in floor.Items)
						{
							AreaConfig current = area;
							bool enabled = AreaView.IsEnabled(current, _config.Defaults);

							<div class="room-row @AreaView.EdgeClass(enabled, LiveStateOf(current))" @key="current">
								@* The switch is the room's power state, so it is a switch and it is on the row where a
								   hand reaches for it. It stays outside the link: a control inside an anchor is neither
								   valid markup nor a thing a thumb can aim at without also navigating. *@
								<button type="button"
										class="switch @(enabled ? "on" : null)"
										role="switch"
										aria-checked="@(enabled ? "true" : "false")"
										aria-label="@($"Automatic lighting in {RoomName(current)}")"
										title="@ToggleTitle(enabled)"
										@onclick="() => ToggleEnabled(current)">
									<span class="switch-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="switch-knob"></span></span>
									<span class="switch-word">@(enabled ? "on" : "off")</span>
								</button>

								@if (RoomLink(current) is { Length: > 0 } href)
								{
									<a class="room-open" href="@href">
										<span class="area-title">@RoomName(current)</span>
										<code class="area-slug">@current.AreaId</code>
										<span class="area-summary-hint">@HouseView.RoomSummary(current)</span>
										<span class="room-open-chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
									</a>
								}
								else
								{
									@* No page to open: either the room names no Home Assistant area, or it does but has
									   not been saved yet, and the room page reads the file rather than this document. *@
									<span class="room-open room-open-shut">
										<span class="area-title">@RoomName(current)</span>
										@if (current.AreaId is { Length: > 0 } pending)
										{
											<code class="area-slug">@pending</code>
											<span class="area-summary-hint">save to open this room</span>
										}
										else
										{
											@* A room that names no area has no address, so its own page cannot exist and
											   everything on it would be out of reach. The picker is the way back: name the
											   area and the room becomes an ordinary room with an ordinary page. Only a
											   hand-edited document reaches this state — every path that proposes a room
											   proposes it with an area id — but a document that reaches it must not become
											   uneditable. *@
											<span class="badge bad">no area</span>
											<span class="area-summary-hint">@HouseView.RoomSummary(current)</span>
											<span class="room-adopt">
												<AreaPicker Value="@current.AreaId"
															ValueChanged="@(areaId => AdoptArea(current, areaId))"
															Areas="@_addableAreas"
															NoneLabel="(pick this room's area)"
															EmptyNote="@AddEmptyNote" />
											</span>
											<button type="button" class="danger room-drop" @onclick="() => RemoveArea(current)">Remove</button>
										}
									</span>
								}
							</div>

							@* What the switch above just did, said under the row it belongs to rather than in a dialog
							   in front of it. A sibling of the row, not a child: the row is a switch beside a link, and
							   a block of chips inside it would break both. *@
							@if (SwitchOnNoteFor(current) is { } switchOn)
							{
								<SwitchOnNoteView Note="@switchOn" Dismissed="@(() => DismissSwitchOnNote(current))" />
							}
						}
					</div>
				}

				@if (_config.Areas.Count == 0)
				{
					<div class="empty">No rooms yet. Add one to get started.</div>
				}

				@* Where the board's footer thread lands. The rooms are in the list above with their own switches, so
				   this line counts them rather than listing them a second time. *@
				@if (HouseView.SwitchedOffLine(_config.Areas, _config.Defaults) is { Length: > 0 } switchedOff)
				{
					<p class="rooms-off">@switchedOff</p>
				}
			}
			else if (_section == Section.Schedule)
			{
				<header class="section-head">
					<h2>Schedule</h2>
					<p class="lede">
						How bright and how warm the lights are through the day. One schedule for the whole house.
					</p>
				</header>

				<section class="panel room-card house-card">
					<div class="room-card-head">
						<Icon Name="@Glyph.Schedule" Class="ic-head" />
						<span class="room-card-title">Through the day</span>
						<span class="room-card-note">one rhythm for the whole house</span>
					</div>

					<div class="house-chart">
						<DaylightChart Periods="@_config.Periods" Location="@_homeLocation" />
					</div>

					@* Blending belongs to the schedule, not to a timing grab-bag: it is entirely about what happens at
					   the boundaries the chart above draws. One token rather than a switch beside a number — blending
					   off and blending over nothing are the same instruction. *@
					<div class="room-sentences">
						<SentenceView Sentences="@_blendSentence"
									  Edited="@OnGlobalEdited"
									  Note="@TokenNote"
									  ShowLegend="false" />
					</div>
				</section>

				<PeriodsEditor Periods="@_config.Periods"
							   Changed="@Revalidate"
							   HouseModeOptions="@_houseModeOptionValues"
							   HouseMode="@_config.Global.HouseMode" />
			}
			else if (_section == Section.HouseModes)
			{
				@* House modes are their own room now, not a group buried in Global. One dropdown helper carries the
				   house's modes; each option is tagged with what it means to the engine. Every binding and handler
				   here is exactly the one that lived under Global — SetHouseModeEntity, the live-options catalog, and
				   OnHouseModeOptionsChanged — moved verbatim. *@
				<header class="section-head">
					<h2>House modes</h2>
					<p class="lede">
						The house mode (Normal, Sleep, Away, Guest) lives in a Home Assistant dropdown helper. Pick the
						helper, then say what each option means. You switch modes on the <a href="/">board</a>.
					</p>
				</header>

				<div class="panel">
					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">House mode
								<InfoPopover Label="House mode">
									Create the helper in Home Assistant: <strong>Settings → Devices &amp; services → Helpers →
									Create helper → Dropdown.</strong> Add your options, save, then pick it here. Home Assistant
									owns the helper, so it survives restarts.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control">
							<EntityPicker Value="@_config.Global.HouseMode?.Entity"
										  ValueChanged="@SetHouseModeEntity"
										  Options="@_inputSelects"
										  NoneLabel="(none — the house has no mode select)"
										  EmptyNote="@EmptyNote("No dropdown helpers yet — create one in Home Assistant (Settings → Devices & services → Helpers), or type an id.")"
										  Placeholder="input_select.husmodus" />
						</div>
						<div class="field-help">
							Say below what each option of the helper means.
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				@* What the modes actually do, in one sentence each, above the field-by-field editor that changes
				   everything else about them. The two values a house actually tunes — how long it must be still
				   before a mode arms itself, and the grace in which your own leaving doesn't cancel it — are
				   tokens here; the rest is prose, because a scene and a period are picked from what Home
				   Assistant has rather than from a shortlist. *@
				@if (_modeLines.Count > 0)
				{
					<section class="panel room-card house-card">
						<div class="room-card-head">
							<Icon Name="@Glyph.Modes" Class="ic-head" />
							<span class="room-card-title">What each mode does</span>
							<span class="room-card-note">@_config.Global.HouseMode?.Entity</span>
						</div>

						<div class="house-rules">
							@foreach (ModeLine line in _modeLines)
							{
								ModeLine mode = line;

								<div class="house-rule">
									<b class="house-rule-name">@mode.Name</b>
									<SentenceView Sentences="@mode.Sentences"
												  Edited="@OnModeEdited"
												  Note="@TokenNote"
												  ShowLegend="false" />
									@if (IsActiveMode(mode))
									{
										<span class="house-rule-now">active now</span>
									}
								</div>
							}
						</div>
					</section>
				}

				@* The option cards render at section level, not inside a form .field-control, so they stretch to the
				   full width the period cards use rather than shrinking to their content. *@
				@if (_config.Global.HouseMode?.Entity is { Length: > 0 })
				{
					<header class="section-head subsection">
						<h3>Mode options
							<InfoPopover Label="Mode options">
								Each option means one thing: Normal, Sleep, Away or Guest. Picking a kind shows only that
								kind's settings. Exactly one option should be Normal — it's what the house returns to.
							</InfoPopover>
						</h3>
					</header>

					<HouseModeOptions HouseMode="@_config.Global.HouseMode"
									  LiveOptions="@_liveSelectOptions"
									  ActiveValue="@_activeModeValue"
									  Periods="@_config.Periods"
									  Scenes="@_scenes"
									  PresenceSensors="@_presenceEntities"
									  DateTimes="@_dateTimes"
									  ActivationEntities="@_activationEntities"
									  NameResolver="@Name"
									  KnownResolver="@Catalog.Knows"
									  IsReady="@HomeAssistantIsAnswering"
									  Changed="@OnHouseModeOptionsChanged" />
				}
			}
			else
			{
				@*
					House holds the settings there is exactly one of.

					That is the membership rule, and it is what settles an argument this page got wrong once. The
					original rule was "a setting lives under the noun it changes", which put the all-rooms defaults
					and the discovery labels under Areas because they change how rooms behave. But that test does
					not discriminate: the schedule changes how rooms behave, so do the house modes, and both have
					sections of their own. The owner's test does — Areas is a LIST OF ROOMS, and these are
					singular. There is exactly one Defaults block and exactly one of each label and device-class
					setting in the whole document; a per-room exclude label does not exist. So they are here, with
					the house's name, its people, the master switch and the fine tuning.

					It was also a findability bug: in a house of seventeen rooms across six floor groups, both sat
					far below the fold of a list nobody scrolls to the bottom of. They lead this section now for
					the same reason — they are the two most substantial things in it.

					Schedule and House modes keep their own sections because they are big and distinct, not
					because the rule excludes them.

					Behind one fold at the bottom, never in a section of its own, are the internals. "Advanced" as
					a place answered "how confident do I need to be?"; it never answered "where do I look?".
				*@
				<header class="section-head">
					<h2>House</h2>
					<p class="lede">
						The settings there is one of: what every room starts with, which lights count as a room's,
						what this house is called, who lives in it, and the one switch that pauses everything.
					</p>
				</header>

				@* --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
				   What every room starts with, in the same two layers the room page uses: the sentences, then
				   the same settings again as rows. First in the section, with the discovery labels under it: one
				   says how a room behaves by default, the other says which lights that reaches, and they are read
				   together.
				   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
				<section class="panel room-card house-card">
					<div class="room-card-head">
						<Icon Name="@Glyph.Areas" Class="ic-head" />
						<span class="room-card-title">Every room starts with these</span>
						<span class="room-card-note">a room's own settings win</span>
					</div>

					<div class="room-sentences">
						<SentenceView Sentences="@AreaSentences.ForDefaults(_config.Defaults)"
									  Edited="@OnDefaultEdited"
									  Note="@TokenNote"
									  ShowLegend="false" />
					</div>

					<div class="room-morebar">
						<button type="button"
								class="room-more"
								aria-expanded="@(_showAllRooms ? "true" : "false")"
								@onclick="() => _showAllRooms = !_showAllRooms">
							All settings @(_showAllRooms ? "▴" : "▾")
						</button>
						<span class="room-more-note">@HouseView.StrayLine(_config.Areas, Catalog.AreaRegistry)</span>
					</div>

					@if (_showAllRooms)
					{
						<div class="room-groups">
							@foreach (RoomSettingGroup group in RoomSettings.Groups)
							{
								RoomSettingGroup current = group;
								bool open = IsOpen(current);

								<div class="sgroup @(open ? "sgroup-open" : null)">
									<button type="button"
											class="sgroup-head"
											aria-expanded="@(open ? "true" : "false")"
											@onclick="() => Toggle(current)">
										<span class="sgroup-title">@current.Title</span>
										<span class="sgroup-note">@current.Note</span>
										<span class="area-chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
									</button>

									@if (open)
									{
										<div class="sgroup-body">
											@* These values ARE the house, so there is no inherit tag and no road back: a
											   SettingRow with no house text renders neither. *@
											@foreach (RoomSetting setting in current.Settings.Where(item => item.AppliesTo(_config.Defaults)))
											{
												RoomSetting item = setting;

												<SettingRow Label="@item.Label" Help="@item.Help">
													@switch (item.Control)
													{
														case RoomControl.Flag:
															<button type="button"
																	class="switch @(RoomSettings.Flag(null, _config.Defaults, item.Key) ? "on" : null)"
																	role="switch"
																	aria-checked="@(RoomSettings.Flag(null, _config.Defaults, item.Key) ? "true" : "false")"
																	aria-label="@item.Label"
																	@onclick="() => SetHouseFlag(item.Key, !RoomSettings.Flag(null, _config.Defaults, item.Key))">
																<span class="switch-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="switch-knob"></span></span>
															</button>
															break;

														case RoomControl.Choice:
															<div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="@item.Label">
																@foreach (TokenChoice option in RoomSettings.DarknessOptions)
																{
																	TokenChoice choice = option;
																	<button type="button"
																			class="@(IsCurrentChoice(item.Key, choice) ? "cur" : null)"
																			aria-pressed="@(IsCurrentChoice(item.Key, choice) ? "true" : "false")"
																			@onclick="() => SetHouseChoice(item.Key, choice.Value)">@choice.Text</button>
																}
															</div>
															break;

														case RoomControl.Entity:
															<EntityPicker Value="@RoomSettings.Entity(null, _config.Defaults, item.Key)"
																		  ValueChanged="@(value => SetHouseEntity(item.Key, value))"
																		  Options="@_sunEntities"
																		  NoneLabel="(none — the engine needs one)"
																		  EmptyNote="@EmptyNote("No sun entity found — type an id (normally sun.sun).")"
																		  Placeholder="sun.sun" />
															break;

														default:
															<Stepper Value="@RoomSettings.Shown(null, _config.Defaults, item.Key)"
																	 Text="@RoomSettings.Describe(null, _config.Defaults, item.Key)"
																	 Label="@item.Label"
																	 Step="@item.Step"
																	 Min="@item.Min"
																	 Max="@item.Max"
																	 IsOwn="true"
																	 ValueChanged="@(value => SetHouseNumber(item.Key, value))" />

															@if (string.Equals(item.Key, nameof(AreaSettings.LuxBrightnessGamma), StringComparison.Ordinal))
															{
																@* The one setting that is a shape rather than a quantity. The number stays
																   in the stepper where it can be changed; the curve says what it means. *@
																<CurveGlyph Exponent="@RoomSettings.Shown(null, _config.Defaults, item.Key)"
																			Label="@CurvePath.Describe(RoomSettings.Shown(null, _config.Defaults, item.Key))" />
															}
															break;
													}
												</SettingRow>
											}
										</div>
									}
								</div>
							}
						</div>
					}
				</section>

				@* One include label, one exclude label, one motion label, one device-class set — for the whole
				   house, with no per-room twin of any of them, which is what makes this a House setting rather
				   than an Areas one. Folded because it is read once and then forgotten, not because it is
				   frightening. *@
				<GroupFold Title="Finding lights &amp; sensors"
						   Description="Which lights this app may manage, what counts as a sensor, and where the house reads the daylight.">
					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">Only manage lights with (label)
								<InfoPopover Label="Only manage lights with (label)">
									When set, only lights carrying this label are managed. Leave empty to manage every light
									that's found. Lights you list by hand in a room are managed either way.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control">
							<LabelPicker Value="@_config.Global.IncludeLabel"
										 ValueChanged="@SetIncludeLabel"
										 Options="@_labelOptions"
										 NoneLabel="(none — manage every light that's found)"
										 UnknownNote="No light carries this label yet, so no room will resolve. Label the lights in Home Assistant, or clear this."
										 Placeholder="adaptive" />
						</div>
						<div class="field-help">Empty is the usual answer: every light discovery finds is managed.</div>
					</div>

					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">Never touch (label)
								<InfoPopover Label="Never touch (label)">
									Anything in Home Assistant carrying this label is invisible to this app — it is never
									discovered, never commanded, never counted.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control">
							<LabelPicker Value="@_config.Global.ExcludeLabel"
										 ValueChanged="@SetExcludeLabel"
										 Options="@_labelOptions"
										 NoneLabel="(none — nothing is excluded)"
										 UnknownNote="Nothing carries this label, so nothing is excluded — that's fine."
										 Placeholder="adaptive-exclude" />
						</div>
						<div class="field-help">This wins over every other setting, including the include label above.</div>
					</div>

					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">Counts as motion (label)
								<InfoPopover Label="Counts as motion (label)">
									A sensor with this label is treated as a motion sensor whatever its type. For the odd
									sensor Home Assistant classifies as something else.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control">
							<LabelPicker Value="@_config.Global.MotionLabel"
										 ValueChanged="@SetMotionLabel"
										 Options="@_labelOptions"
										 NoneLabel="(none — device classes decide)"
										 UnknownNote="Nothing carries this label yet, so only the device classes below decide."
										 Placeholder="adaptive-motion" />
						</div>
					</div>

					@*
						The outdoor sensor sits here rather than under the all-rooms Darkness group, per ui-design-c §4:
						it is an input the house discovers darkness with, and grouping it with the other "how rooms find
						things" settings leaves one fewer home for sensor-ish settings. A deliberate small deviation from
						area-restructure §3.1, flagged there and again here.
					*@
					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">Outdoor light sensor
								<InfoPopover Label="Outdoor light sensor">
									A house-wide outdoor light sensor. Any room without its own lux sensor uses this to decide
									whether it is dark, instead of falling back to the sun's height — which follows clouds, not
									just the clock. A room with its own lux sensor keeps using that. Leave empty to use sun height.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control">
							<EntityPicker Value="@_config.Global.OutdoorLuxSensor"
										  ValueChanged="@(value => SetOutdoorLux(value))"
										  Options="@_luxSensors"
										  NoneLabel="(none — rooms without their own lux use sun height)"
										  EmptyNote="@EmptyNote("No illuminance sensors found — type an id.")"
										  Placeholder="sensor.ute_lux" />
						</div>
					</div>

					<details class="advanced">
						<summary>Advanced &mdash; what counts as a sensor</summary>

						<div class="field">
							<div class="field-head">
								<label class="field-label">What counts as a motion sensor
									<InfoPopover Label="What counts as a motion sensor">
										Which kinds of binary sensor count as movement. The built-in set (motion, occupancy,
										presence) suits nearly every house. Ticking anything replaces the whole set — include the
										built-ins you still want.
									</InfoPopover>
								</label>
							</div>
							<div class="field-control checks">
								@foreach (string deviceClass in _motionDeviceClassChoices)
								{
									string current = deviceClass;
									<label>
										<input type="checkbox"
											   checked="@_config.Global.MotionDeviceClasses.Contains(current, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)"
											   @onchange="args => ToggleMotionDeviceClass(current, args.Value is true)" />
										@current
									</label>
								}
							</div>
							<div class="field-help">
								Ticking nothing means the built-in set
								(<code>@string.Join(", ", GlobalConfig.DefaultMotionDeviceClasses)</code>), which is usually right.
								Ticking anything <strong>replaces</strong> that set rather than adding to it, so tick every class
								you want &mdash; including the built-in ones you still need. Currently in effect:
								<code>@string.Join(", ", _config.Global.EffectiveMotionDeviceClasses)</code>.
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="field">
							<div class="field-head">
								<label class="field-label">What counts as a light-level sensor
									<InfoPopover Label="What counts as a light-level sensor">
										What counts as a light-level sensor. "illuminance" is nearly always right.
									</InfoPopover>
								</label>
							</div>
							<div class="field-control">
								<select class="cell wide" @bind="_config.Global.IlluminanceDeviceClass">
									@foreach (string deviceClass in _sensorDeviceClasses)
									{
										<option value="@deviceClass">@deviceClass</option>
									}
									@if (!_sensorDeviceClasses.Contains(_config.Global.IlluminanceDeviceClass, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
									{
										<option value="@_config.Global.IlluminanceDeviceClass">@_config.Global.IlluminanceDeviceClass — no sensor here reports this</option>
									}
								</select>
							</div>
							<div class="field-help">What qualifies a sensor as a room's lux source during discovery.</div>
						</div>
					</details>
				</GroupFold>

				<section class="panel room-card house-card">
					<div class="room-card-head">
						<Icon Name="@Glyph.Residents" Class="ic-head" />
						<span class="room-card-title">People</span>
						<span class="room-card-note">whose presence decides Home and Away</span>
					</div>

					<div class="room-sentences">
						<SentenceView Sentences="@_awaySentence"
									  Edited="@OnGlobalEdited"
									  Note="@TokenNote"
									  ShowLegend="false" />
					</div>

					<div class="house-fields">
						<div class="field">
							<div class="field-head">
								<label class="field-label">Who lives here
									<InfoPopover Label="Who lives here">
										Whose presence decides Home and Away. Empty means everyone Home Assistant knows, including
										people added later — usually right. List people only to leave someone out (a car tracker,
										a guest entity).
									</InfoPopover>
								</label>
							</div>
							<div class="field-control">
								<EntityListPicker Value="@_config.Global.Persons"
												  ValueChanged="@SetPersons"
												  Options="@_persons"
												  NameResolver="@Name"
												  KnownResolver="@Catalog.Knows"
												  EmptyLabel="Empty — everyone Home Assistant knows, including people added later."
												  AddLabel="Watch someone…"
												  EmptyNote="@EmptyNote("No people found in Home Assistant — type an id.")"
												  Placeholder="person.example" />
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<section class="panel room-card house-card">
					<div class="room-card-head">
						<Icon Name="@Glyph.House" Class="ic-head" />
						<span class="room-card-title">Master switch</span>
						<span class="room-card-note">one switch pauses everything</span>
					</div>

					@* The switch is picked from whatever Home Assistant has. That deliberately does not care how it got
					   there — a hand-made helper today, or the enable switch NetDaemon's state manager publishes for
					   this app, which is the built-in default when nothing is chosen. *@
					<div class="house-fields">
						<p class="group-note">
							On means lights adjust automatically; off pauses everything at once &mdash; nothing is turned off,
							changes just stop. This is the same switch as the On/Off control on the board.
						</p>
						<div class="field">
							<div class="field-head">
								<label class="field-label">Master switch
									<InfoPopover Label="Master switch">
										One switch that pauses all automatic lighting. Left unset, it uses this app's own enable
										switch in Home Assistant. Point it at your own helper toggle to override.
									</InfoPopover>
								</label>
							</div>
							<div class="field-control">
								<EntityPicker Value="@_config.Global.KillSwitchEntity"
											  ValueChanged="@(value => Set(() => _config.Global.KillSwitchEntity = value))"
											  Options="@_switches"
											  NoneLabel="(default — this app's own enable switch)"
											  EmptyNote="@EmptyNote("No switches or toggle helpers found — type an id.")"
											  Placeholder="input_boolean.adaptive_lighting_enabled" />
							</div>
							@if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_config.Global.KillSwitchEntity))
							{
								@* Defaulted: the built-in switch is by definition an enabled-flag, so the polarity dropdown is
								   hidden and the concrete id is shown instead. *@
								<div class="field-help">
									@if (_config.Global.EffectiveKillSwitchEntity is { Length: > 0 } builtIn)
									{
										<span>
											Using <code>@builtIn</code> — the app's own switch in Home Assistant. Note: turning it
											off pauses the whole app, this page included.
										</span>
									}
									else
									{
										<span>
											Will default to this app's own enable switch once the host has connected to Home Assistant.
										</span>
									}
								</div>
							}
							else
							{
								@* Bound through strings rather than straight to the bool: @bind writes "True"/"False" into
								   the element, neither option matches, and the select renders with nothing chosen — which
								   is exactly the control that must never be ambiguous about which way round it reads. *@
								<div class="field-control">
									<select class="cell wide"
											@bind:get="@(_config.Global.KillSwitchActiveWhenOff ? "true" : "false")"
											@bind:set="SetKillSwitchPolarity">
										<option value="true">read as an enabled flag — off kills the engine</option>
										<option value="false">read as a kill switch — on kills the engine</option>
									</select>
								</div>
								<div class="field-help">Gates the whole engine. This overrides the built-in default switch.</div>
							}
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<GroupFold Title="Fine tuning"
						   Description="The house's name and the internals: clocks, how the engine tells its own changes from a person's, and how close is close enough.">
					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">House name
								<InfoPopover Label="House name">
									A label for logs and notifications, so two houses can be told apart.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control"><input class="cell wide" @bind="_config.ConfigName" placeholder="Adaptive lighting" /></div>
					</div>

					@* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
					   NetDaemonUserId is write-only here, on purpose. It is rendered as set/not-set and never as
					   its value, in either direction — the input starts empty and only overwrites what is stored
					   if something is typed into it. Nothing on this page, and nothing in the markup it sends,
					   ever carries the current value.
					   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">NetDaemon user id
								<InfoPopover Label="NetDaemon user id">
									The Home Assistant user this app connects as (Settings → People, on the token's user).
									Naming it helps tell the app's own light changes from a person's. Optional.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control">
							@if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_config.Global.NetDaemonUserId))
							{
								<span class="badge">not set</span>
								<span class="hint">not set — manual changes are detected by timing alone.</span>
							}
							else
							{
								<span class="badge ok">set</span>
								<span class="hint">set — the value is never shown.</span>
								<button type="button" @onclick="ClearUserId">Clear</button>
							}
						</div>
						<div class="field-control">
							<input class="cell wide" type="password" autocomplete="off" placeholder="type a new value to replace it" @bind="_userIdInput" />
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">Re-check the rooms every
								<InfoPopover Label="Re-check the rooms every">
									How often each room re-checks the time of day and the light outside. Once a minute is plenty.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control">
							<input class="cell num" type="number" min="1" @bind="_config.Global.CircadianTickSeconds" /> <span class="unit">s</span>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">Recognise own changes for
								<InfoPopover Label="Recognise own changes for">
									How long the app's own commands are recognised as its own rather than as a person at a switch.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control">
							<input class="cell num" type="number" min="0" @bind="_config.Global.SelfEchoWindowSeconds" /> <span class="unit">s</span>
						</div>
						<div class="field-help">
							A state change on a light within this window of a command is read as our own echo rather than as a
							human at a switch.
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">Other automations count as manual
								<InfoPopover Label="Other automations count as manual">
									When another automation changes a light here, treat it like a manual change and back off.
									Untick only if this app should always win.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control checks">
							<label><input type="checkbox" @bind="_config.Global.TreatAutomationsAsManual" /> a change from another automation counts as a manual override</label>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="field">
						<div class="field-head">
							<label class="field-label">Close enough
								<InfoPopover Label="Close enough">
									A light already this close to its target is left alone. Without a margin, lights would be
									endlessly re-corrected over invisible differences.
								</InfoPopover>
							</label>
						</div>
						<div class="field-control">
							<span class="hint">brightness</span>
							<input class="cell num" type="number" step="0.5" min="0" @bind="_config.Global.BrightnessTolerancePct" /> <span class="unit">%</span>
							<span class="hint">colour</span>
							<input class="cell num" type="number" step="10" min="0" @bind="_config.Global.ColorTempToleranceKelvin" /> <span class="unit">K</span>
						</div>
					</div>
				</GroupFold>

				@* Where the file lives and whether the engine is running: the two facts that explain why an edit here
				   did or did not reach the house. Below the settings because they are read once, when something is
				   wrong, rather than on every visit. *@
				<GroupFold Title="This installation"
						   Description="Where the document lives, and whether the engine is running.">
					<div class="status-row">
						<span class="status-label">Engine</span>
						@if (Engine.IsRunning)
						{
							<span class="badge ok">running</span>
							<span>@Engine.RunningAreaCount @(Engine.RunningAreaCount == 1 ? "room" : "rooms") managed.</span>
						}
						else
						{
							<span class="badge bad">not running</span>
							<span>@(Engine.Fault ?? "Not started yet.")</span>
						}
					</div>
					<div class="status-row">
						<span class="status-label">File</span>
						<code>@Location.Path</code>
						<span class="badge">@Describe(Location.Source)</span>
						@if (Engine.Store.HasBackup)
						{
							<span class="hint">previous version kept at <code>@Engine.Store.BackupPath</code></span>
						}
					</div>
					<div class="status-row">
						<span class="status-label">Home Assistant</span>
						@if (HomeAssistantIsAnswering)
						{
							<span class="badge ok">connected</span>
							<span>@_areas.Count @(_areas.Count == 1 ? "area" : "areas") known.</span>
						}
						else
						{
							<span class="badge bad">not connected</span>
							<span>You can still type ids by hand; name checks are skipped if you save now.</span>
						}
					</div>
				</GroupFold>
			}
		</div>
	</div>
}

@code {
	/// <summary>
	///     The page's four sections. One is shown at a time; the rail switches.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Four, not five: a setting now lives under the noun it changes, so there is no <c>Defaults</c> beside
	///     <c>Areas</c> (the all-rooms settings <i>are</i> area settings, and are a card inside it) and no
	///     <c>Advanced</c> at all (its contents went to the section whose noun they change, each behind that
	///     section's own fold). "Advanced" answered "how confident must I be?"; it never answered "where do I look?".
	/// </remarks>
	private enum Section
	{
		Areas,
		Schedule,
		HouseModes,
		House
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     What every token popover on this page promises.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     There is no default for this in <c>SentenceView</c> on purpose: only the page knows whether a pick waits
	///     behind a save bar or applies by itself, and a promise about saving is the worst kind of thing to be
	///     wrong about. This page waits, and names its own undo.
	/// </remarks>
	private const string TokenNote = "Applies when you save. Discard changes puts it back.";

	private AdaptiveLightingConfig _config = new();
	private ValidationResult? _liveValidation;
	private SaveResult? _result;
	private string? _loadError;
	private string? _userIdInput;
	private bool _busy;
	private Section _section = Section.Areas;

	// A successful save is confirmed transiently: the message shows, then clears itself after a few seconds, so
	// the save bar does not carry a stale "Engine rebuilt" indefinitely. Validation errors and failed saves are
	// NOT transient — they render through _liveValidation / _result and stay until resolved.
	private string? _saveConfirmation;
	private CancellationTokenSource? _confirmationCts;

	// The registry is read once per page load rather than once per area: the lists are identical for every
	// area, and asking Home Assistant for every light once per area per render is how a page with six areas
	// becomes slow for no reason.
	private IReadOnlyList<AreaOption> _areas = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<AreaOption> _addableAreas = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> _luxSensors = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> _sunEntities = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> _persons = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> _switches = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> _inputSelects = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> _scenes = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> _motionSensors = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> _presenceEntities = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> _dateTimes = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<EntityOption> _activationEntities = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<string> _liveSelectOptions = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<string> _houseModeOptionValues = [];
	private string? _activeModeValue;
	private (double Latitude, double Longitude)? _homeLocation;
	private IReadOnlyList<LabelOption> _labelOptions = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<string> _sensorDeviceClasses = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<string> _motionDeviceClassChoices = [];

	// The sentences, rebuilt with the document rather than during render: a projection asked for once per
	// keystroke on a page that re-renders on every keystroke is work nobody sees.
	private IReadOnlyList<Sentence> _blendSentence = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<Sentence> _awaySentence = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<ModeLine> _modeLines = [];

	// The room list, grouped by the floor each area sits on. Recomputed whenever the document changes rather than
	// during render: grouping asks the registry for a floor per area, and Blazor re-renders this page on every
	// keystroke in it.
	private IReadOnlyList<FloorGroup<AreaConfig>> _areaGroups = [];

	// What each room is called, by reference, recomputed with the document for the same reason the groups are.
	private readonly Dictionary<AreaConfig, string> _roomNames = [];

	// What each room is doing right now, for the row's edge colour, keyed by area id with a name fallback — the
	// same join the snapshot cache itself uses. Read from the cache rather than subscribed to: this page is an
	// editor, and a live re-render mid-edit buys a livelier edge at the cost of the thing being edited.
	private IReadOnlyDictionary<string, AreaState> _liveStates = new Dictionary<string, AreaState>(StringComparer.Ordinal);

	// The re-setup flow. Null means the panel is closed; otherwise these are the rooms it opens with ticked —
	// every room from the section header, one from inside a room's page.
	private IReadOnlyCollection<string>? _setupScope;
	private string? _setupNote;

	// Adding a room: which area it will be, and the ids added since the last save. A room the file does not
	// have yet cannot be opened, because the room page reads the document from disk rather than from here.
	private bool _addingRoom;
	private string? _addAreaId;
	private readonly HashSet<string> _unsaved = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

	// What each room's switch had to say when it was last turned on, and which of those have been read. Per visit
	// rather than persisted: this is advice about the house as it is right now, not a preference.
	private readonly Dictionary<string, SwitchOnNote> _switchOnNotes = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);
	private readonly HashSet<string> _switchOnRead = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

	// The all-rooms detail view: revealed on demand, and the sections a reader has opened or closed inside it.
	private bool _showAllRooms;
	private readonly HashSet<string> _closedGroups = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);
	private readonly HashSet<string> _openedGroups = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

	/// <summary>
	///     Whether Home Assistant is actually answering, as opposed to merely not throwing.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     These are different, and the difference matters. <see cref="HaCatalog.IsHomeAssistantReady"/> reports
	///     that a read threw, which is what NetDaemon's state cache does before its first connection completes.
	///     But the <i>registry</i> does not throw when it has nothing — it answers with an empty list. So a host
	///     that cannot reach Home Assistant at all renders a page where every picker is empty, nothing has
	///     thrown, and the status panel would cheerfully say nothing is wrong. Checking that something actually
	///     came back is the only signal that covers both.
	/// </remarks>
	private bool HomeAssistantIsAnswering =>
		Catalog.IsHomeAssistantReady && (_areas.Count > 0 || _luxSensors.Count > 0 || _switches.Count > 0);

	/// <summary>
	///     Which section to open on load, taken from the URL (<c>?section=housemodes</c>). Lets another page
	///     deep-link straight to a room: the board's "set up a house mode" link lands on House modes rather
	///     than the default Areas. An absent or unrecognised value leaves the default. Read once on load — after
	///     that the section rail owns which room is showing.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter]
	[SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "section")]
	public string? SectionQuery { get; set; }

	/// <inheritdoc/>
	protected override void OnInitialized()
	{
		Reload();

		if (Resolve(SectionQuery) is { } requested)
			_section = requested;
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     The section a <c>?section=</c> value names, or <c>null</c> when it names none.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     The renamed sections answer to their old names as well. A bookmark or a phone shortcut is somebody's
	///     saved intent, and every restructure so far moved contents rather than deleting them — landing somewhere
	///     with no explanation would look like the link broke. Each alias points at wherever its contents are now,
	///     which is the only rule this list has: <c>defaults</c> follows the all-rooms settings to House, where
	///     they went when House took over the settings there is exactly one of; <c>advanced</c> and <c>people</c>
	///     were already there.
	/// </remarks>
	private static Section? Resolve(string? query)
	{
		if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(query))
			return null;

		if (Enum.TryParse(query, ignoreCase: true, out Section requested) && Enum.IsDefined(requested))
			return requested;

		return query.Trim().ToLowerInvariant() switch
		{
			"periods" => Section.Schedule,
			"defaults" => Section.House,
			"rooms" => Section.Areas,
			"advanced" => Section.House,
			"people" => Section.House,
			_ => null
		};
	}

	private void Show(Section section) => _section = section;

	private string? ActiveClass(Section section) => _section == section ? "active" : null;

	private void Reload()
	{
		_result = null;
		_userIdInput = null;
		CloseSetup();
		CloseAdd();
		_unsaved.Clear();
		_setupNote = null;

		try
		{
			_config = Engine.Store.Load();
			_loadError = null;
		}
		catch (LightingConfigException exception)
		{
			_loadError = exception.Message;
			return;
		}

		LoadCatalog();
		Revalidate();
	}

	private void StartFromScratch()
	{
		_config = new AdaptiveLightingConfig();
		_loadError = null;
		LoadCatalog();
		Revalidate();
	}

	private void LoadCatalog()
	{
		// The document has just been re-read, so the registry is re-read with it: the catalog caches discovery
		// per load, and a stale answer next to a fresh document would be the worst of both.
		Catalog.Invalidate();

		// Areas are labelled with what discovery resolves in each — lights, motion, lux — so the conventions
		// discovery reads have to come from the document being edited rather than from a default.
		_areas = Catalog.Areas(_config.Global);
		_labelOptions = Catalog.LabelOptions();
		_sunEntities = Catalog.EntitiesInDomains("sun");
		_persons = Catalog.EntitiesInDomains("person", "device_tracker");
		_switches = Catalog.EntitiesInDomains("input_boolean", "switch");
		_inputSelects = Catalog.EntitiesInDomains("input_select");
		_scenes = Catalog.EntitiesInDomains("scene");
		_dateTimes = Catalog.DateTimeEntities();
		_activationEntities = Catalog.EntitiesInDomains("input_boolean", "switch", "binary_sensor");
		_liveSelectOptions = Catalog.SelectOptionsOf(_config.Global.HouseMode?.Entity);
		_activeModeValue = Catalog.CurrentStateOf(_config.Global.HouseMode?.Entity);
		_houseModeOptionValues = HouseModeOptionValues();
		_homeLocation = HomeGeo.TryRead();
		_sensorDeviceClasses = Catalog.SensorDeviceClasses();

		// Offer what discovery would accept, so an explicit list is a correction to discovery rather than a
		// parallel universe of every binary sensor in the house.
		_motionSensors = Catalog.EntitiesWithDeviceClass("binary_sensor", [.. _config.Global.EffectiveMotionDeviceClasses]);
		_luxSensors = Catalog.EntitiesWithDeviceClass("sensor", [_config.Global.IlluminanceDeviceClass]);

		// The presence-reset override picker: the motion sensors discovery would find, plus person entities — the
		// same set 09 §5.1 specifies, so a household can name a person's arrival or a specific sensor's turn-on.
		_presenceEntities = [.. _motionSensors.Concat(Catalog.EntitiesInDomains("person"))];

		// The union of what this house reports and what the engine defaults to: a household that has no
		// occupancy sensor today may buy one tomorrow, and the built-in classes must stay tickable.
		_motionDeviceClassChoices =
		[
			.. Catalog.BinarySensorDeviceClasses()
				.Concat(GlobalConfig.DefaultMotionDeviceClasses)
				.Distinct(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
				.OrderBy(deviceClass => deviceClass, StringComparer.Ordinal)
		];
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Re-reads the document's own verdict on itself, and with it everything the page derives from the
	///     document: its sentences, which floor each room is on, what each room is doing, and which areas are
	///     still free to add.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Every edit already routes through here, so hanging the derived views off it means they can never be one
	///     edit behind what is on screen — and it costs a dictionary of at most a few dozen rooms.
	/// </remarks>
	private void Revalidate()
	{
		_liveValidation = Engine.Validate(_config);
		_blendSentence = [HouseSentences.Blend(_config.Global)];
		_awaySentence = [HouseSentences.AwayDebounce(_config.Global)];
		_modeLines = HouseSentences.Modes(_config.Global.HouseMode, _config.Periods);
		_addableAreas = HouseView.Unconfigured(_areas, _config.Areas);
		RegroupAreas();
		RefreshLiveStates();
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Groups the rooms by floor through the helper the board shares, so the two pages can never disagree.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     A registry that has not connected throws rather than answering, and a settings page that refused to
	///     render its rooms because Home Assistant was still starting would be useless exactly when somebody is
	///     trying to fix that. One unnamed group is the floorless degradation the design already specifies, so the
	///     failure mode is simply today's flat list.
	/// </remarks>
	private void RegroupAreas()
	{
		try
		{
			_areaGroups = FloorGrouping.Group(_config.Areas, area => area.AreaId, Catalog.AreaRegistry);
		}
		catch (InvalidOperationException)
		{
			_areaGroups = _config.Areas.Count == 0 ? [] : [new FloorGroup<AreaConfig>(null, [.. _config.Areas])];
		}

		// Names come from the same registry read as the floors, and stale for exactly as long: until the next edit.
		_roomNames.Clear();

		foreach (AreaConfig area in _config.Areas)
			_roomNames[area] = HouseView.DisplayName(area, Catalog.AreaRegistry);
	}

	/// <summary>The live state of each room the cache has heard from, keyed by area id and falling back to its name.</summary>
	private void RefreshLiveStates()
	{
		Dictionary<string, AreaState> states = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

		foreach (AreaSnapshot snapshot in Cache.Snapshots)
			states[snapshot.AreaId is { Length: > 0 } areaId ? areaId : snapshot.AreaName] = snapshot.State;

		_liveStates = states;
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     What a room is doing right now, or <c>null</c> when nothing has been heard about it.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     By area id first, because ids do not change while a page is open and display names do. The name fallback
	///     covers a room configured with explicit entities and no area id, and events from builds published before
	///     snapshots carried an id.
	/// </remarks>
	private AreaState? LiveStateOf(AreaConfig area)
	{
		if (area.AreaId is { Length: > 0 } areaId && _liveStates.TryGetValue(areaId, out AreaState byId))
			return byId;

		return _liveStates.TryGetValue(RoomName(area), out AreaState byName) ? byName : null;
	}

	// ===================== the room list =====================

	/// <summary>
	///     A room's own page, or <c>null</c> when there is nothing to open yet.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     A room with no Home Assistant area has no route at all. A room that has one but was added since the
	///     last save has no page <i>yet</i>: the room page loads the document from disk, so following the link
	///     would land on "no such room" and read as a broken list rather than as an unsaved edit.
	/// </remarks>
	private string? RoomLink(AreaConfig area) =>
		area.AreaId is { Length: > 0 } areaId && !_unsaved.Contains(areaId) ? HouseView.RoomHref(areaId) : null;

	/// <summary>
	///     What a room is called in this list.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Resolved through <see cref="HouseView.DisplayName(AreaConfig, IAreaRegistry)"/> — which is
	///     <see cref="AreaNaming"/>'s order with this page's own placeholder on the end — so a room that states no
	///     name of its own reads as "Kjøkken" rather than as <c>kjokken</c>, and reads the same here as it does on
	///     the board, in the log and on its own page.
	///     <para>
	///         Read from the map built with the document rather than asked per render, for the reason the floor
	///         groups are: each row asks three times, this page re-renders on every keystroke, and the registry
	///         <i>throws</i> rather than answering until Home Assistant has connected. A room added since the last
	///         rebuild falls back to the document alone, which is one render behind and never wrong.
	///     </para>
	/// </remarks>
	private string RoomName(AreaConfig area) =>
		_roomNames.TryGetValue(area, out string? name) ? name : HouseView.DisplayName(area, null);

	private static string ToggleTitle(bool enabled) => enabled
		? "On — the lights in this room are managed. Switch off to leave them alone."
		: "Off — this room is watched but its lights are never changed.";

	/// <summary>
	///     Flips a room's power switch.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Writes an explicit <c>true</c> or <c>false</c>, never <c>null</c>: inheritance stays for documents that
	///     predate the switch, but a decision somebody just made with their thumb belongs in the file as itself.
	///     And it is an edit like any other — it arms the save bar rather than saving.
	/// </remarks>
	private void ToggleEnabled(AreaConfig area)
	{
		bool switchingOn = !AreaView.IsEnabled(area, _config.Defaults);
		area.Enabled = switchingOn;
		Revalidate();

		if (switchingOn)
			RaiseSwitchOnNote(area);
		else
			_switchOnNotes.Remove(KeyOf(area));
	}

	// ===================== what switching a room on will command =====================

	/// <summary>
	///     Works out what a room now commands and, when there is something to say, says it under the room's row.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     <para>
	///         Advisory and after the fact — see <see cref="SwitchOnWarning"/>. The switch has already taken
	///         effect; nothing here can stop it, and a room switched on by <i>Switch on this floor</i> deliberately
	///         raises nothing, because six notes at once is a wall rather than a warning.
	///     </para>
	///     <para>
	///         Computed on the press rather than during render. It runs the engine's own resolver over the room,
	///         which reads a state per candidate entity — per-render that would be a page that stutters for text
	///         nobody has asked for yet.
	///     </para>
	/// </remarks>
	private void RaiseSwitchOnNote(AreaConfig area)
	{
		string key = KeyOf(area);

		// Somebody who has read this room's note and flipped its switch twice has not asked the question twice.
		if (_switchOnRead.Contains(key))
			return;

		SwitchOnNote? note = SwitchOnWarning.For(
			RoomName(area),
			Catalog.LightsIn(area, _config.Defaults, _config.Global),
			_config.Global.IncludeLabel);

		if (note is not null)
			_switchOnNotes[key] = note;
	}

	private SwitchOnNote? SwitchOnNoteFor(AreaConfig area) => _switchOnNotes.GetValueOrDefault(KeyOf(area));

	/// <summary>Puts one room's note away for the rest of this visit.</summary>
	private void DismissSwitchOnNote(AreaConfig area)
	{
		string key = KeyOf(area);

		_switchOnNotes.Remove(key);
		_switchOnRead.Add(key);
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     How a room is keyed while the page is open: its area id, or its position for a row that names none.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     A document may carry a room with no area id at all — only a hand-edited file reaches that state, but it
	///     must still be able to hold a note of its own rather than share one with every other nameless row.
	/// </remarks>
	private string KeyOf(AreaConfig area) =>
		area.AreaId is { Length: > 0 } areaId ? areaId : $"#{_config.Areas.IndexOf(area)}";

	/// <summary>What the floor's bulk action offers: switching a floor off once every room on it is already on.</summary>
	private string FloorAction(FloorGroup<AreaConfig> floor) =>
		AreaView.AllEnabled(floor.Items, _config.Defaults) ? "Switch off this floor" : "Switch on this floor";

	/// <summary>
	///     Switches every room on a floor, as an edit: explicit values written into the document, save bar armed,
	///     nothing written to disk.
	/// </summary>
	private void SwitchFloor(FloorGroup<AreaConfig> floor)
	{
		AreaView.SwitchAll(floor.Items, !AreaView.AllEnabled(floor.Items, _config.Defaults));
		Revalidate();
	}

	private void ToggleAddRoom()
	{
		if (_addingRoom)
			CloseAdd();
		else
		{
			_setupNote = null;
			_addingRoom = true;
		}
	}

	private void CloseAdd()
	{
		_addingRoom = false;
		_addAreaId = null;
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Adds a room for one Home Assistant area.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Switched off explicitly, exactly as discovery proposes rooms: software that starts commanding lights in
	///     a room the moment it is named is the first experience this design spent a work package removing.
	///     <para>
	///         <b>The area's name is not copied in.</b> It used to be, because a nameless room rendered as its
	///         slug; the name is now resolved from the registry wherever it is shown, so writing it would freeze
	///         the room at whatever the area happened to be called today and stop a rename in Home Assistant ever
	///         arriving. What is written is what discovery writes — an area id and a switch — and nothing else.
	///     </para>
	/// </remarks>
	private void AddRoom(string? areaId)
	{
		if (areaId is not { Length: > 0 })
			return;

		CloseAdd();

		_config.Areas.Add(new AreaConfig { AreaId = areaId, Enabled = false });
		_unsaved.Add(areaId);
		_setupNote = $"{AreaName(areaId)} added, switched off. Save and apply, then open it to set it up.";

		Revalidate();
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Gives a room with no Home Assistant area one, which is also what gives it a page.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Marked unsaved like a newly added room: the room page reads the document from disk, so the link only
	///     works once the file says the same thing this page does. The area's name is not copied in, for the same
	///     reason it is not in <see cref="AddRoom"/> — the row now reads its name from the registry.
	/// </remarks>
	private void AdoptArea(AreaConfig area, string? areaId)
	{
		if (areaId is not { Length: > 0 })
			return;

		area.AreaId = areaId;
		_unsaved.Add(areaId);
		Revalidate();
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Drops a room the list cannot open.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Removing a room is the room page's own act, at the bottom of the page past everything it would throw
	///     away. A room with no Home Assistant area has no page, so it would otherwise be unreachable and
	///     unremovable — old documents that list entities explicitly and name no area are exactly that case.
	/// </remarks>
	private void RemoveArea(AreaConfig area)
	{
		_config.Areas.Remove(area);
		Revalidate();
	}

	// ===================== the all-rooms settings =====================

	/// <summary>
	///     Whether a section of the detail view is open. Sections carry their own default and a reader's toggle
	///     outranks it, so the rare sections stay folded until somebody wants them and stay open once they did.
	/// </summary>
	private bool IsOpen(RoomSettingGroup group) =>
		group.StartsOpen ? !_closedGroups.Contains(group.Title) : _openedGroups.Contains(group.Title);

	private void Toggle(RoomSettingGroup group)
	{
		if (IsOpen(group))
		{
			_openedGroups.Remove(group.Title);
			_closedGroups.Add(group.Title);
		}
		else
		{
			_closedGroups.Remove(group.Title);
			_openedGroups.Add(group.Title);
		}
	}

	private bool IsCurrentChoice(string key, TokenChoice choice) =>
		string.Equals(RoomSettings.Choice(null, _config.Defaults, key).ToString(), choice.Value, StringComparison.Ordinal);

	/// <summary>
	///     Applies one edit from the all-rooms sentences.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     A key this page cannot apply is a sentence and a page that have drifted apart. Silently doing nothing
	///     would look like a dead control, so it is logged where it can be found.
	/// </remarks>
	private void OnDefaultEdited(SentenceEdit edit)
	{
		if (!RoomSettings.Apply(_config.Defaults, edit))
		{
			Logger.LogWarning("A sentence offered an edit to {Key}, which the House tab does not know how to apply.", edit.Key);
			return;
		}

		Revalidate();
	}

	private void SetHouseNumber(string key, double value)
	{
		RoomSettings.SetShown(_config.Defaults, key, value);
		Revalidate();
	}

	private void SetHouseFlag(string key, bool value)
	{
		RoomSettings.SetFlag(_config.Defaults, key, value);
		Revalidate();
	}

	private void SetHouseChoice(string key, string value)
	{
		if (!Enum.TryParse(value, out DarknessSource source))
			return;

		_config.Defaults.Darkness = source;
		Revalidate();
	}

	private void SetHouseEntity(string key, string? value)
	{
		RoomSettings.SetEntity(_config.Defaults, key, value);
		Revalidate();
	}

	// ===================== the house's own sentences =====================

	/// <summary>
	///     Applies an edit from a house-wide sentence — the schedule's blend and the away debounce.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Blending is one value in two properties: zero minutes means the lights step at the boundary, which is
	///     what <c>SmoothTransitions</c> off already meant. The minute count is left alone when blending is
	///     switched off, so turning it back on restores what it was rather than a zero nobody chose.
	/// </remarks>
	private void OnGlobalEdited(SentenceEdit edit)
	{
		switch (edit.Key)
		{
			case nameof(GlobalConfig.BlendMinutes):
				int minutes = edit.Integer;
				_config.Global.SmoothTransitions = minutes > 0;

				if (minutes > 0)
					_config.Global.BlendMinutes = minutes;

				break;

			case nameof(GlobalConfig.AwayDebounceMinutes):
				_config.Global.AwayDebounceMinutes = edit.Minutes;
				break;

			default:
				Logger.LogWarning("A house sentence offered an edit to {Key}, which the House tab does not know how to apply.", edit.Key);
				return;
		}

		Revalidate();
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Applies an edit from a mode sentence.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     The key carries which option it belongs to, because a house has several modes on one card. It is read
	///     back by <see cref="HouseSentences.TryReadModeKey"/> rather than parsed here, so both halves of the
	///     encoding stay in one file.
	/// </remarks>
	private void OnModeEdited(SentenceEdit edit)
	{
		if (!HouseSentences.TryReadModeKey(edit.Key, out int index, out string property)
			|| _config.Global.HouseMode is not { } houseMode
			|| index < 0
			|| index >= houseMode.Options.Count)
		{
			Logger.LogWarning("A mode sentence offered an edit to {Key}, which names no option on this document.", edit.Key);
			return;
		}

		HouseModeOptionConfig option = houseMode.Options[index];

		switch (property)
		{
			case nameof(HouseModeOptionConfig.ActivateAfterNoMotionMinutes):
				option.ActivateAfterNoMotionMinutes = edit.Minutes;
				break;

			case nameof(HouseModeOptionConfig.ResetPresenceGraceMinutes):
				option.ResetPresenceGraceMinutes = edit.Minutes;
				break;

			default:
				Logger.LogWarning("A mode sentence offered an edit to {Property}, which the House tab does not know how to apply.", property);
				return;
		}

		Revalidate();
	}

	/// <summary>Whether the house is standing on this mode right now, as Home Assistant reports it.</summary>
	private bool IsActiveMode(ModeLine mode) =>
		_activeModeValue is { Length: > 0 } active
		&& string.Equals(active.Trim(), mode.Name.Trim(), StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

	// ===================== setting rooms up again =====================

	/// <summary>
	///     Whether setting rooms up again can be offered: it rebuilds each room from the registry, so a registry
	///     that is not answering means the button would produce an empty plan and a confusing dialog.
	/// </summary>
	private bool CanSetUpAgain => HomeAssistantIsAnswering && _setupScope is null;

	private void OpenSetupForEveryRoom()
	{
		_setupNote = null;
		CloseAdd();
		_setupScope = [.. _config.Areas.Select(area => area.AreaId).OfType<string>().Where(areaId => areaId.Length > 0)];
	}

	private void CloseSetup() => _setupScope = null;

	/// <summary>Plans a setup run for the panel. Pure — nothing is changed until the plan is confirmed.</summary>
	private SetupPlan PlanSetup(IReadOnlyCollection<string> scope) => Catalog.PlanSetup(_config, scope);

	/// <summary>
	///     Carries out a confirmed setup run.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     The mutation is the engine's own <see cref="AreaSetupService.Apply"/>, so a rebuild from this page and a
	///     rebuild on first start are the same code. It touches the in-memory document only: the save bar is armed
	///     exactly as any other edit arms it, <em>Discard changes</em> is still the undo, and the one write path
	///     stays the one write path.
	/// </remarks>
	private void ApplySetup(SetupPlan plan)
	{
		AreaSetupService.Apply(_config, plan);
		CloseSetup();

		List<string> done = [];

		if (plan.Rebuilds.Count > 0)
			done.Add($"{Rooms(plan.Rebuilds.Count)} rebuilt");

		if (plan.NewAreas.Count > 0)
		{
			done.Add($"{Rooms(plan.NewAreas.Count)} added, switched off");

			foreach (AreaConfig added in plan.NewAreas)
			{
				if (added.AreaId is { Length: > 0 } areaId)
					_unsaved.Add(areaId);
			}
		}

		_setupNote = done.Count == 0
			? "Nothing changed."
			: $"{string.Join(" · ", done)}. Nothing is written yet — press Save and apply, or Discard changes to undo.";

		Revalidate();

		static string Rooms(int count) => $"{count} {(count == 1 ? "room" : "rooms")}";
	}

	/// <summary>The name Home Assistant shows for an area id, for rooms the document does not have yet.</summary>
	private string AreaName(string areaId) =>
		_areas.FirstOrDefault(area => string.Equals(area.Id, areaId, StringComparison.Ordinal))?.Name ?? areaId;

	// ===================== the write path =====================

	private void Save()
	{
		_busy = true;

		// Drop any lingering success toast up front, so a refused save is never shown next to a stale confirmation.
		_confirmationCts?.Cancel();
		_saveConfirmation = null;

		try
		{
			// Only now, and only if something was typed. An untouched box must leave the stored value alone —
			// the page never had it to give back.
			if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_userIdInput))
			{
				_config.Global.NetDaemonUserId = _userIdInput.Trim();
				_userIdInput = null;
			}

			// Read the file's timestamp before the save so we can confirm the write actually advanced it — the
			// reassurance is grounded in the file changing on disk, not merely in the call returning.
			DateTimeOffset? writtenBefore = Engine.Store.LastWrittenUtc;

			_result = Engine.Save(_config);
			_liveValidation = _result.Validation;

			if (_result.Written)
			{
				// Confirm the bytes landed: re-read the on-disk timestamp and show it. A successful Written save
				// always advances it; showing the concrete time is what turns "I pressed save" into "it is saved".
				DateTimeOffset? writtenAfter = Engine.Store.LastWrittenUtc;
				string savedAt = writtenAfter is { } utc ? utc.ToLocalTime().ToString("HH:mm:ss") : "now";
				bool advanced = writtenAfter is { } a && (writtenBefore is not { } b || a >= b);

				// The success message is transient: hand it to the toast and clear _result so the persistent
				// note never restates it. A refused/failed save keeps _result and shows it until resolved.
				ShowSaveConfirmation(advanced
					? $"Saved ✓  Written to {System.IO.Path.GetFileName(Engine.Store.FilePath)} at {savedAt}."
					: "Saved, but the file timestamp did not change — check the log.");
				_result = null;

				// Everything on disk now has a page to open.
				_unsaved.Clear();
				_setupNote = null;

				// Re-read so the page shows what is actually on disk, formatting and all, rather than the
				// object it happened to be holding.
				_config = Engine.Store.Load();
				LoadCatalog();
				Revalidate();
			}
		}
		catch (LightingConfigException exception)
		{
			_loadError = exception.Message;
		}
		finally
		{
			_busy = false;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Shows the success confirmation, then clears it after a few seconds. Fire-and-forget: the delay must not
	///     block the save. A fresh token supersedes any in-flight timer, so rapid saves never leave an early clear
	///     hiding a later confirmation.
	/// </summary>
	private void ShowSaveConfirmation(string message)
	{
		_saveConfirmation = message;

		_confirmationCts?.Cancel();
		_confirmationCts?.Dispose();
		_confirmationCts = new CancellationTokenSource();
		CancellationToken token = _confirmationCts.Token;

		_ = DismissConfirmationAfterDelay(token);
	}

	private async Task DismissConfirmationAfterDelay(CancellationToken token)
	{
		try
		{
			await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(3.5), token).ConfigureAwait(false);
		}
		catch (TaskCanceledException)
		{
			return;
		}

		_saveConfirmation = null;
		await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged).ConfigureAwait(false);
	}

	/// <inheritdoc/>
	public void Dispose()
	{
		_confirmationCts?.Cancel();
		_confirmationCts?.Dispose();
	}

	// ===================== the small print =====================

	private void ClearUserId()
	{
		_config.Global.NetDaemonUserId = null;
		Revalidate();
	}

	private void Set(Action mutate)
	{
		mutate();
		Revalidate();
	}

	/// <summary>Which way round the master switch reads, written from the segment the select stands on.</summary>
	private void SetKillSwitchPolarity(string? value) =>
		Set(() => _config.Global.KillSwitchActiveWhenOff = string.Equals(value, "true", StringComparison.Ordinal));

	private void SetOutdoorLux(string? value) =>
		Set(() => _config.Global.OutdoorLuxSensor = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value) ? null : value);

	private void SetIncludeLabel(string? value) => SetLabel(() => _config.Global.IncludeLabel = value);

	// Exclude and motion are non-nullable in the model — they ship with a default label — so "none" is written as
	// an empty string rather than a null. Empty is what "no label" already meant to the resolver: no entity can
	// carry a nameless label, so nothing matches, which is the whole meaning of switching the field off.
	private void SetExcludeLabel(string? value) => SetLabel(() => _config.Global.ExcludeLabel = value ?? string.Empty);

	private void SetMotionLabel(string? value) => SetLabel(() => _config.Global.MotionLabel = value ?? string.Empty);

	/// <summary>
	///     Writes a label field and rebuilds what depends on it.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Discovery reads these labels, so an area's "2 lights, 1 motion, 1 lux" summary is only true for the
	///     labels that were in effect when it was counted. The catalog's own discovery cache notices the change by
	///     itself (its key includes the labels); the area list is a snapshot this page took, so it has to be retaken
	///     — the same reason the motion device classes rebuild it.
	/// </remarks>
	private void SetLabel(Action mutate)
	{
		mutate();
		_areas = Catalog.Areas(_config.Global);
		Revalidate();
	}

	private void SetPersons(IReadOnlyList<string> persons) =>
		Set(() => _config.Global.Persons = [.. persons]);

	/// <summary>The option values a period's "sets mode" dropdown may reference: live ∪ configured, deduped.</summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<string> HouseModeOptionValues() =>
	[
		.. _liveSelectOptions
			.Concat(_config.Global.HouseMode?.Options.Select(option => option.Value) ?? [])
			.Where(value => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value))
			.Select(value => value.Trim())
			.Distinct(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
	];

	private void SetHouseModeEntity(string? value)
	{
		if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value))
		{
			// Leave the object (its option tags may still be wanted); ConfigNormalizer drops it on save if empty.
			if (_config.Global.HouseMode is { } existing)
				existing.Entity = null;
		}
		else
		{
			// Lazily created the first time an entity is picked, so a never-adopted document acquires no block.
			(_config.Global.HouseMode ??= new HouseModeConfig()).Entity = value;
		}

		_liveSelectOptions = Catalog.SelectOptionsOf(_config.Global.HouseMode?.Entity);
		_activeModeValue = Catalog.CurrentStateOf(_config.Global.HouseMode?.Entity);
		_houseModeOptionValues = HouseModeOptionValues();
		Revalidate();
	}

	private void OnHouseModeOptionsChanged()
	{
		_houseModeOptionValues = HouseModeOptionValues();
		Revalidate();
	}

	private void ToggleMotionDeviceClass(string deviceClass, bool on)
	{
		if (on)
		{
			if (!_config.Global.MotionDeviceClasses.Contains(deviceClass, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
				_config.Global.MotionDeviceClasses.Add(deviceClass);
		}
		else
		{
			_config.Global.MotionDeviceClasses.RemoveAll(existing =>
				string.Equals(existing, deviceClass, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));
		}

		// The motion picker's contents depend on this, so it has to be rebuilt rather than left showing the
		// entities the previous answer would have found. So do the area labels: discovery reads these classes,
		// so an area's motion count is only true for the set that was in effect when it was counted. The presence
		// override picker is built from the motion sensors too, so it goes stale the same way if not rebuilt.
		_motionSensors = Catalog.EntitiesWithDeviceClass("binary_sensor", [.. _config.Global.EffectiveMotionDeviceClasses]);
		_presenceEntities = [.. _motionSensors.Concat(Catalog.EntitiesInDomains("person"))];
		_areas = Catalog.Areas(_config.Global);
		Revalidate();
	}

	private string Name(string entityId) => Catalog.FriendlyNameOf(entityId) ?? entityId;

	/// <summary>
	///     The empty-picker note, chosen by whether Home Assistant is actually answering. This page knows the
	///     reason a list is empty and the component does not: when connected, an empty domain gets an honest
	///     "you have none of these yet — here's how to make one" (<paramref name="noneYet"/>); when not, the
	///     note names the real cause instead of blaming a healthy connection.
	/// </summary>
	private string EmptyNote(string noneYet) => HomeAssistantIsAnswering
		? noneYet
		: "Waiting for Home Assistant — type an id for now.";

	/// <summary>Why the add-a-room picker has nothing to offer: no connection, or no area left unclaimed.</summary>
	private string AddEmptyNote => !HomeAssistantIsAnswering
		? "Waiting for Home Assistant — type an area id for now."
		: "Every area Home Assistant knows already has a room. Type an id if one is coming.";

	private static string Describe(ConfigLocationSource source) => source switch
	{
		ConfigLocationSource.External => "outside the deploy folder — survives a deploy",
		ConfigLocationSource.SeededFromTree => "created from the shipped example — survives a deploy",
		_ => "inside the deploy folder — a deploy will overwrite it"
	};
}
